Falsifies `machine_discovery::riscv64::verification::a_multi_letter_extension_is_never_read_as_a_privilege_letter`.

The defect: the privilege-letter scan runs over the whole ISA string instead of
stopping at the first `_`.

`riscv,isa` puts single-letter extensions and privilege letters in one undelimited
run, then multi-letter extensions after the first `_`. QEMU `virt` writes `_sstc`,
`_svinval` and `_sdtrig` today; the mainline JH7110 dtsi writes `_smstateen`. A
scan that reaches past the delimiter reads any of those leading `s` bytes as the
hart claiming supervisor mode.

What that costs is on record at the bench. The VisionFive 2's vendor firmware marks
its M/U-only S7 monitor core `"okay"` with `mmu-type = "riscv,sv39"`, both false,
and starting it crashed the firmware (2026-08-14, notes/visionfive2.md). The hart's
own `riscv,isa` is the only thing that tells the truth, and `Some(false)` from this
function is the only thing `Cpu::is_startable` has to refuse it with. Turn a
`Some(false)` into a `Some(true)` and this kernel starts a core with no supervisor
mode, on the machine where that already happened once.

Expected to fail: the `assert_eq!` that appending `_sstc` changes nothing, on a run
spelling `u` and no `s`.

